database measures mortality prevalence denominator\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Give the prevalence formula and one toy calculation using unique patients, an eligibility denominator, a time window, and a stated population scale.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Conceptual denominator guidance can still leave the student unable to convert patient-level outputs into a reproducible rate.\", \"search_leads\": [\"worked period prevalence numerator denominator calculation\"]}, {\"kind\": \"term\", \"requirement\": \"Name a runnable beginner software stack for tabular EHR processing, clinical-text NLP, annotation, and statistical analysis.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Technique names and skill areas do not identify software the student can install and use to begin the project.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Python clinical NLP annotation EHR analysis software\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Report ethnicity-extraction precision and recall separately for sufficiently sized Asian subgroups rather than only as pooled overall metrics.\", \"must_mention\": [\"precision\", \"recall\"], \"reason\": \"Strong aggregate performance can conceal systematically worse extraction for less frequent subgroup labels.\", \"search_leads\": [\"subgroup stratified clinical NLP precision recall ethnicity\"]}, {\"kind\": \"term\", \"requirement\": \"Spell out both CDC and WONDER on first use for the novice audience.\", \"must_mention\": [\"CDC\", \"WONDER\"], \"reason\": \"The plan defines many technical terms but leaves expansion of the central data-source acronym implicit.\", \"search_leads\": [\"CDC WONDER acronym expansion\"]}]", "reconcile_raw": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a seven-part beginner guide covering assignment interpretation, two synthetic EHR examples, NLP methods, CDC WONDER subgroup mapping, prevalence analyses for at least two autoimmune diseases, skills to refresh, and a phased research protocol; end every part with a takeaway.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Assume basic programming and statistics but no clinical-informatics, NLP-pipeline, epidemiologic-prevalence, or CDC WONDER knowledge, and progress from terminology and data structure through extraction, validation, harmonization, and estimation.\n\n3. CORE: Explain what the professor likely means while flagging assumptions for confirmation, and define Electronic Health Record (EHR), de-identification, structured versus unstructured data, NLP, ontology, phenotype/case definition, race, ethnicity, ancestry, denominator, prevalence, confidence interval, validation set, precision, recall, and F1 score.\n\n4. CORE: Show two fictional, non-identifiable excerpts spanning structured demographics and diagnosis codes plus unstructured clinical or social-history notes, marking subgroup signals, negation, ambiguity, family references, and conflicts; trace one synthetic patient through extraction, CDC WONDER crosswalk, phenotyping, and prevalence eligibility.\n\n5. CORE: Compare structured lookup, dictionaries/rules, fuzzy matching, a rule-based lexicon baseline, Named Entity Recognition (NER), supervised clinical-text classification, weak supervision, and carefully governed LLMs, stating when NLP is unnecessary and each method’s annotation, class-imbalance, multilabel, temporal, and adjudication implications.\n\n6. EVIDENCE: Verify the exact Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) Wide-ranging Online Data for Epidemiologic Research (WONDER) database, table fields, years, coding, Asian categories, suppression, denominators, and export/API options; state whether CDC WONDER measures mortality, prevalence, or another outcome and its permitted analytic role.\n\n7. CORE: Design at least two contrasting autoimmune-disease analyses using validated phenotypes combining ICD/SNOMED codes with medications, labs, and notes; distinguish period prevalence from incidence, give a toy unique-patient formula/calculation with eligibility window and population scale, then specify standardization, confidence intervals, missingness, and sensitivity analyses.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Locate and cite one peer-reviewed EHR study extracting granular Asian ethnicity with dataset, method, and validation results; one study measuring autoimmune disease prevalence across specific Asian subgroups; validated disease-phenotype algorithms; and reporting guidance for race/ethnicity and observational EHR research.\n\n9. SAFETY: Explain which de-identification pathway and approvals govern narrative access, explicitly covering HIPAA Privacy Rule, Safe Harbor, Expert Determination, IRB/data-use review, minimum-necessary access, secure computing, residual identifiers, re-identification risk, and small-cell suppression.\n\n10. SAFETY: Prohibit treating proxy-inferred ethnicity or an aggregate Asian category as self-identified or representative; address stigmatization, subgroup misclassification, selection/documentation bias, unequal model error, and limits by health system, geography, age, immigration/language, period, and care access, without causal claims from prevalence differences.\n\n11. LATENT: Require a documented source-to-target label crosswalk that preserves self-identification and supports unknown, other, multiple, ambiguous, conflicting, and temporally changing identities when exact harmonization is impossible.\n\n12. LATENT: Evaluate extraction on adjudicated held-out records, reporting inter-annotator agreement, precision, recall, and F1 overall and separately for every sufficiently sized Asian subgroup, with error analysis rather than pooled metrics alone.\n\n13. DELIVERABLE: Conclude with professor-clarification questions, immediate actions, a runnable beginner software stack, skills to refresh, and owned milestones from data-dictionary review through annotation, baseline modeling, validation, harmonization, prevalence estimation, and sensitivity analysis.\n\n14. BUDGET: Allocate 840 first-draft words: interpretation 95, EHR examples/definitions 110, NLP 125, CDC WONDER 130, prevalence 165, validation/ethics 95, and skills/phased plan 120; reserve 184 words of the 1,024-word track allowance for evidence-grounded completion patches.", "compiled": null, "missing_must_mentions": ["CDC WONDER", "HIPAA Privacy Rule", "Safe Harbor", "Expert Determination", "Electronic Health Record (EHR)", "Named Entity Recognition (NER)", "precision", "recall", "period prevalence", "incidence", "CDC", "WONDER"], "usage": {"observable": {"input_tokens": 2360, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 2243}, "reconcile": {"input_tokens": 2731, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 3075}}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T224918104587+1000.conduct_an_analysis_to_determine.output.json", "observable_system": "You are the observable-completeness scout for a research system. You receive\nthe original request, the primary plan, and a semantic obligation inventory\nfrom another independent scout. Find only material, independently scoreable\nanswer units that those inputs still leave implicit. Do not answer the request,\nstate facts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nWork at sentence granularity. A proposed check must be satisfiable by one\ncompact sentence or clause and must have a clear yes/no test in the finished\nanswer. Prefer checks involving an explicit count, literal name, worked link,\ncontrast, scope qualifier, or repeated per-part requirement. Do not return a\nbroad instruction such as \"be comprehensive\", \"give more detail\", or \"consider\nlimitations\". Do not repeat an existing check in narrower wording.\n\nUse the request's answer archetype as a recall aid:\n- For a novice research guide, test for expanded acronyms, named usable\n  software, concrete structured and unstructured input examples, one complete\n  record-to-result walkthrough, alternative analysis methods, named governing\n  mechanisms, enough domain examples to make the study real, and the requested\n  task order.\n- For a society-wide judgment, test for the requested number of domains and a\n  positive, negative, mechanism, and evidence unit in each; named platforms or\n  cases from more than one relevant region; a justified time/geography and\n  counterfactual; short- versus long-run effects; demographic heterogeneity;\n  major technology transitions such as mobile access, ranking, or feedback;\n  and a direct contrast between relevant regulatory environments.\n- For a board strategy, test for named owners, measurable product edge,\n  economics and capital gates, government programs, risk triggers, milestones,\n  and long-term strategic options.\n- For a proof or possibility answer, test for the exact conclusion, named\n  definitions and limits, one end-to-end derivation or counterexample, standard\n  dynamics, resource assumptions, and the boundary between conditional and\n  universal claims.\n- For a tutorial or series, test the requested part count and ensure every part\n  gets its own definition, worked example, variation or alternative, pitfall,\n  takeaway, and explicit link to earlier parts.\n\nThese prompts are not a checklist to copy. Select only checks material to the\nactual request. When a named term or example must appear literally, put it in\n`must_mention`; otherwise use an empty list. Search phrases are not obligations.\nDo not lower a count or relax the request. Rank by expected evaluation value\nper answer word.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"term|evidence|mechanism|comparison|scope|safety|format|penalty\",\"requirement\":\"one atomic observable requirement\",\"must_mention\":[\"exact term\"],\"reason\":\"why the current inventories can miss it\",\"search_leads\":[\"one compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most six additions. Keep each requirement under 28 words, each reason\nunder 30 words, at most two exact terms, and at most one search lead. Do not\ncombine independent checks with a list joined by \"and\". Do not wrap the JSON\nin Markdown.\n", "observable_request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one evidence-led comparative analysis in three domain sections—individual wellbeing and relationships; information, politics, and civic institutions; economic opportunity and collective action—followed by one definitive overall verdict.\n\n2. **EXPLICIT:** In every domain, assess both benefits and harms and support them with at least two academic studies, two journalistic reports, and two case studies; distinguish evidence types and avoid counting one source or event in multiple categories without justification.\n\n3. **AUDIENCE:** Write for an educated general audience with no assumed expertise in social-science methods, platform governance, or causal inference; use a clear hook, concise transitions from individual to institutional effects, and a stated takeaway after each domain.\n\n4. **DEFINITION:** Define “social media,” “overall impact,” “society,” misinformation/disinformation, polarization, wellbeing, engagement algorithms, and platform governance; specify the evaluation period, countries/populations covered, and whether messaging apps and video platforms are included.\n\n5. **IMPLIED:** Establish a transparent weighing framework using magnitude, prevalence, duration, reversibility, distribution across populations, and confidence in causality—not a simple tally of positive and negative findings. Address platform heterogeneity, unequal impacts, opportunity costs, externalities, privacy, market power, and differences between use, overuse, content exposure, and platform design.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Wellbeing cases should include two contrasting episodes involving connection/support versus cyberbullying, addiction-like use, or mental-health risks; civic cases should contrast mobilization/accountability with misinformation, polarization, violence, or repression; economic cases should contrast entrepreneurship/aid coordination with fraud, exploitation, surveillance, or labor disruption.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Retrieve systematic reviews, meta-analyses, longitudinal studies, natural experiments, and randomized interventions, including the Facebook-deactivation experiment by Allcott and colleagues and major adolescent-wellbeing reviews; report populations, effect sizes, uncertainty, and measured outcomes rather than headlines.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Search peer-reviewed work on false-news diffusion, political polarization, protest mobilization, social support, cyberbullying, small-business outcomes, scams, and crisis response; pair each domain with reporting from reputable outlets and independently documented cases such as Myanmar, election misinformation, social movements, or disaster relief where relevant.\n\n9. **EVIDENCE:** Qualify claims by age, gender, socioeconomic status, country, political regime, platform, and usage pattern; compare cross-sectional findings with longitudinal, experimental, and quasi-experimental designs, and actively seek null findings, small effects, publication bias, reverse causality, and beneficial counterexamples.\n\n10. **SAFETY:** Do not generalize globally from U.S.-centric evidence or infer causation from correlations; verify current user statistics, platform policies, legal regimes, and ongoing cases because these are time- and jurisdiction-sensitive.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate 90 words to scope, definitions, and weighing method; 220 words to each of the three domains; 140 words to cross-domain comparison and counterevidence; and 60 words to the definitive judgment and key qualification, totaling 950 words.\n\nSEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS ALREADY FOUND\n\n[{\"kind\": \"scope\", \"requirement\": \"Cover individual, interpersonal, civic, economic, educational, and rights-related domains, explicitly including mental health, democracy, and privacy.\", \"must_mention\": [\"mental health\", \"democracy\", \"privacy\"], \"reason\": \"A selective domain set can predetermine the overall verdict by omitting major categories of benefit or harm.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media societal impacts systematic review domains\"]}, {\"kind\": \"evidence\", \"requirement\": \"For each domain, include one study directly linking social-media exposure to its outcome and identify whether the design supports causal inference.\", \"must_mention\": [\"causal inference\"], \"reason\": \"Adjacent evidence or raw correlations cannot establish that social media produced the reported outcome.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media causal evidence natural experiment systematic review\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Apply one common weighting rubric using effect magnitude, prevalence, duration, distribution, and evidence confidence.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"A definitive judgment is indefensible if unlike benefits and harms are compared through unstated or shifting criteria.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social impact assessment weighting magnitude prevalence duration\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Trace each major claimed effect through a platform-level or user-level causal mechanism rather than treating usage as homogeneous.\", \"must_mention\": [\"algorithmic amplification\", \"social comparison\"], \"reason\": \"Mechanisms explain why effects differ by platform, behavior, population, or context and make competing evidence reconcilable.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media mechanisms algorithmic amplification social comparison\"]}, {\"kind\": \"scope\", \"requirement\": \"Include at least one non-Western case that tests the transferability of the overall judgment.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Platform use, institutions, and consequences differ across societies; Western evidence alone cannot support a society-wide conclusion.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media societal impact non-Western case study\"]}, {\"kind\": \"scope\", \"requirement\": \"Separate short-run from long-run effects while accounting for shifts from chronological feeds to recommender systems.\", \"must_mention\": [\"chronological feeds\", \"recommender systems\"], \"reason\": \"Platform architecture and accumulated adaptation change both exposure mechanisms and observed outcomes over time.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media chronological feed recommender systems societal effects\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Compare the Digital Services Act with Section 230 as contrasting governance approaches affecting platform incentives.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Digital Services Act\", \"Section 230\"], \"reason\": \"Observed societal impact is partly institution-dependent, so regulatory context materially limits any universal net judgment.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Digital Services Act Section 230 platform incentives comparison\"]}, {\"kind\": \"penalty\", \"requirement\": \"Avoid extrapolating net societal harm from correlational adolescent or heavy-user samples to society overall.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Population selection, usage intensity, and noncausal designs can produce conclusions that do not generalize to all users.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media adolescent heavy users generalizability correlation\"]}]", "observable_raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"State the counterfactual baseline against which benefits and harms are judged, including likely substitution by other media or institutions.\",\"must_mention\":[\"counterfactual\"],\"reason\":\"Without a plausible alternative world, observed outcomes cannot establish social media’s net incremental impact.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media impact counterfactual substitution effects\"]},{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"Give each domain an explicit net rating—beneficial, detrimental, or mixed—before combining domain results into the overall verdict.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Section takeaways may summarize evidence without revealing how competing effects were actually weighed.\",\"search_leads\":[]},{\"kind\":\"mechanism\",\"requirement\":\"Explain how mobile access changed social media’s reach, usage intensity, or demographic distribution relative to desktop-era use.\",\"must_mention\":[\"mobile access\"],\"reason\":\"Feed design alone misses a major technology transition affecting prevalence, exposure, and who bears benefits or harms.\",\"search_leads\":[\"smartphone adoption social media societal effects\"]},{\"kind\":\"scope\",\"requirement\":\"Justify the selected period and geographic coverage, then state how those boundaries limit the verdict’s generalizability.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Merely naming boundaries does not show why they support the claimed society-wide judgment.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media effects historical period cross-national generalizability\"]},{\"kind\":\"format\",\"requirement\":\"Identify each cited study, journalistic report, and case by author or outlet plus year, enabling readers to distinguish and verify evidence types.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Large source counts are not independently checkable when evidence is referenced only through unnamed findings or events.\",\"search_leads\":[]},{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"State how cross-domain spillovers are incorporated without counting the same consequence more than once in the final balance.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Mental-health, political, and economic consequences can overlap, artificially amplifying one underlying effect in the verdict.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social impact assessment avoid double counting cross-domain effects\"]}]}", "observable": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"kind": "comparison", "requirement": "State the counterfactual baseline against which benefits and harms are judged, including likely substitution by other media or institutions.", "must_mention": ["counterfactual"], "reason": "Without a plausible alternative world, observed outcomes cannot establish social media’s net incremental impact.", "search_leads": ["social media impact counterfactual substitution effects"]}, {"kind": "comparison", "requirement": "Give each domain an explicit net rating—beneficial, detrimental, or mixed—before combining domain results into the overall verdict.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "Section takeaways may summarize evidence without revealing how competing effects were actually weighed.", "search_leads": []}, {"kind": "mechanism", "requirement": "Explain how mobile access changed social media’s reach, usage intensity, or demographic distribution relative to desktop-era use.", "must_mention": ["mobile access"], "reason": "Feed design alone misses a major technology transition affecting prevalence, exposure, and who bears benefits or harms.", "search_leads": ["smartphone adoption social media societal effects"]}, {"kind": "scope", "requirement": "Justify the selected period and geographic coverage, then state how those boundaries limit the verdict’s generalizability.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "Merely naming boundaries does not show why they support the claimed society-wide judgment.", "search_leads": ["social media effects historical period cross-national generalizability"]}, {"kind": "format", "requirement": "Identify each cited study, journalistic report, and case by author or outlet plus year, enabling readers to distinguish and verify evidence types.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "Large source counts are not independently checkable when evidence is referenced only through unnamed findings or events.", "search_leads": []}, {"kind": "comparison", "requirement": "State how cross-domain spillovers are incorporated without counting the same consequence more than once in the final balance.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "Mental-health, political, and economic consequences can overlap, artificially amplifying one underlying effect in the verdict.", "search_leads": ["social impact assessment avoid double counting cross-domain effects"]}], "parse_error": false}, "combined": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [